subsection:: Melodic

method:: bass
method:: bass2
method:: pad
method:: pad2
method:: chords
method:: chords2
method:: lineIn
method:: updateTimbre

code::
g = CaosGear.new;
g.bass(out: \reverb, semitoneArray: [48, 50, 52, 55]);
g.pad(waveform: 'tri');

// one chord: fixed timbre
g.chords(chordsArray: #['m']);

// several chords: sequence or random per trigger
g.chords(chordsArray: #['m', 'M7'], seqType: 'seq');
g.chords2(chordsArray: #['M', 'M9'], seqType: 'rand');
::

note:: code::waveform:: chooses the pad SynthDef variant when the instrument is
created. code::chordsArray:: is a chord sequence: one element keeps a fixed
chord, several elements advance (code::seqType: 'seq'::) or are chosen at random
(code::seqType: 'rand'::) on each trigger. Both the note line and the chord
sequence use code::seqType::. To override the current chord manually, use
link::Classes/CaosInstrument#-updateTimbre::. code::set(\chord, ...):: does not
change the SynthDef.
::

method:: updateTimbre
Shortcut for the last instrument built. It is equivalent to calling
code::updateTimbre:: on the object returned by code::pad::, code::chords:: or
code::chords2::. The receiver stays a code::CaosGear::, so it can still be used
to build or query.
code::
g = CaosGear.new;
p = g.pad(waveform: 'sin');
g.updateTimbre(waveform: 'tri'); // updates p, returns g

c = g.chords(chordsArray: #['m']);
g.updateTimbre(chord: 'M7');
::

note:: In code::pad::, the code::pulse:: variant reuses code::iphase:: as the
pulse width: SuperCollider's code::Pulse:: has no phase argument. A value of 0
leaves a flat, silent wave, so CaosBox changes it to 0.25. To choose another
width, pass for example code::iphase: 0.1::. ::

